    "q-183692": {
      "dom_id": "step-q183692",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$",
      "worked_solution": "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
